  "full_text": "Think a probate letter is genuine because it has a HMCTS number on it? Think again. Scammers can make letters look very official. They may [music] use a deceased person's real details, an actual case reference number, and even a spoof telephone number that appears [music] legitimate. Look at these two letters. Exact same information, but one is for 11,000 and one is for 110 million. And this is how easy it is for scammers to alter information. Here are some steps you should take [music] to check if a probate letter is genuine. Step one, check the sender and details carefully. >> [music] >> Look for spelling mistakes, unusual email addresses, or unexpected payment requests. Watch out for pressure to provide your date of birth, bank details, [music] passwords, or security codes, or demands for an upfront fee to release an inheritance. Step two, if there is a contact number printed on the letter, don't automatically trust it. The number may be fake. >> [music] >> It could connect to an automated message which scammers can use to impersonate HMCTS in a follow-up call. >> [music] >> Stop, check, verify before you provide information. Step three, >> [music] >> to properly verify the letter, visit gov.uk's probate service. Contact them independently to confirm whether [music] the letter is genuine. Never use the contact details provided in a suspicious letter. And remember, never make a payment or give personal information until you've independently verified the letter. Still unsure if a probate letter is genuine? Don't take any chances. Contact Fraser and Fraser for expert guidance and peace of mind, >> [music] >> completely free of charge.",
